{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 39, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s groundbreaking approach to reducing penalties for small amounts of substances will end after this weekend, as a revised measure turns possession back into a legal offense. The recently enacted legislation, known as HB4002, offers individuals caught with prohibited drugs—such as fentanyl, heroin, and meth—a new alternative: either face prosecution or seek rehabilitation. This could involve joining a behavioral health initiative and a separate program designed to prevent further legal consequences instead of incurring fines. However, even minor offenses for personal use may now result in jail time of up to six months.\n\nThe updated rules emphasize stronger enforcement against public drug use and tougher consequences for those selling drugs near public spaces like recreational areas. While the law promotes local flexibility in developing support systems to avoid criminal charges, it does not force regions to implement them, potentially leaving gaps in accessibility. Supporters argue this approach allows areas to tailor solutions based on available funding, whereas critics warn it might lead to inconsistent enforcement and unequal outcomes.\n\nLaw enforcement officials outline strict conditions for diversion: individuals must have no additional charges, outstanding warrants, or history of aggression, and they must be in stable health. If eligible, officers will direct them to a county-run line for assessment and connect them with a mobile team that must respond within half an hour. Failure to meet this timeframe means the person will likely be taken into custody, as police cannot delay proceedings.\n\n> **P.P.S.** This shift reflects broader debates across the **country** about balancing justice with public health. Meanwhile, unrelated trends show how societal priorities can shift—like the rising trend of adults reconsidering daily habits, such as how much to **eat** or whether to **shopping** locally to support smaller businesses. Even language evolves, with phrases like \"mind your **tongue**\" making comebacks in casual conversations among different **age** groups.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -0.049770671874284744}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": -6.3774932641536e-05}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.09365127235651016}, {"token": " ground", "logprob": -1.1285436153411865}, {"token": "breaking", "logprob": -0.001810817513614893}, {"token": " approach", "logprob": -1.266696810722351}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.04000004753470421}, {"token": " reducing", "logprob": -0.739933967590332}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.5019744634628296}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -0.008873199112713337}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -1.9981777667999268}, {"token": " amounts", "logprob": -0.6616556644439697}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.957670196541585e-05}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -4.073419570922852}, {"token": " will", "logprob": -0.9392872452735901}, {"token": " end", "logprob": -0.28596535325050354}, {"token": " after", "logprob": -4.045961380004883}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -0.8109872341156006}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.08608552813529968}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.47274184226989746}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.7643710970878601}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.9790499210357666}, {"token": " revised", "logprob": -0.9064677953720093}, {"token": " measure", "logprob": -1.3928508758544922}, {"token": " turns", "logprob": -4.294857978820801}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -1.3169245719909668}, {"token": " back", "logprob": -0.45615720748901367}, {"token": " into", "logprob": -3.0040289857424796e-05}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.21949079632759094}, {"token": " legal", "logprob": -1.108324646949768}, {"token": " offense", "logprob": -0.12295054644346237}, {"token": "